# Reverse proxy and TLS (X-01, #63). Caddy obtains and renews Let's Encrypt certificates itself;
# ports 80 and 443 must be open and SITE_HOST / API_HOST must resolve to this server.
{
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

# Security headers of every response on both hosts (X-06, #160): HSTS here, where TLS ends, and
# nosniff also for what the applications do not answer themselves (static assets, resource routes).
# HSTS lasts a year, without includeSubDomains and preload: other subdomains of the zone may have no
# HTTPS, and the preload list is hard to leave, so both are the owner's call. The CSP and the other
# headers come from the web server (src/server/securityHeaders.server.ts) and the API
# (Infrastructure/SecurityHeadersSetup.cs).
(security_headers) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		-Server
	}
}

# The site: server-rendered pages and static assets, robots.txt, the sitemaps and
# /indexnow-key.txt (all of them routes of the web server).
{$SITE_HOST} {
	import security_headers
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy web:3000
}

# The API, same-site with the site: the refresh cookie (SameSite=Strict, Path=/auth) works as is.
{$API_HOST} {
	import security_headers
	encode zstd gzip
	# Uploads are capped at 10 MB by the API (Uploads:MaxBytes); refuse anything larger here.
	request_body {
		max_size 12MB
	}
	reverse_proxy api:8080
}

# Optional: www.mktba.ru -> mktba.ru. Uncomment once DNS has the www record, otherwise Caddy keeps
# failing to get its certificate.
